LEA with a 32-bit destination computes the address with 32-bit adds

In 64-bit mode `lea 0x1(%rax), %r8d` was a 64-bit add followed by a 32-bit move to clear the upper half.
The destination only keeps the low 32 bits, which 32-bit adds compute the same, and a 32-bit result is
already zero-extended: one `add w2, w8, #1`. wasm2c's i32 arithmetic is made of these. Segment
prefixes stay ignored as before; the address-size prefix (32-bit addressing) gives the same result.

squark's tools/wasm-bench/leatest (632 cases of 32-, 16- and 64-bit LEA, base, index, scale,
displacements that wrap, RIP-relative, addr32) passes before and after. FBW A380X benchmark: 2.630M ->
2.577M instructions per tick (-2%), cycles within noise.
diff --git a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/OpcodeDispatcher.cpp b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/OpcodeDispatcher.cpp
--- a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/OpcodeDispatcher.cpp
+++ b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/OpcodeDispatcher.cpp
@@ -92,6 +92,15 @@
                                                                           OpSize::i32Bit;
   } else {
     DstSize = OpAddr == X86Tables::DecodeFlags::FLAG_OPERAND_SIZE_LAST ? OpSize::i16Bit : OpSize::i32Bit;
+  }
+
+  if (Is64BitMode && DstSize == OpSize::i32Bit && !Op->Src[0].IsGPR()) {
+    // A 32-bit destination only keeps the low half of the address, which 32-bit adds get right, and their result is
+    // already zero-extended: `add w2, w8, #1` instead of a 64-bit add and a 32-bit move.
+    AddressMode A = DecodeAddress(Op, Op->Src[0], MemoryAccessType::DEFAULT, true /* IsLoad */);
+    A.Segment = nullptr;
+    StoreResultGPR_WithOpSize(Op, Op->Dest, LoadEffectiveAddress(this, A, OpSize::i32Bit, false), DstSize);
+    return;
   }
 
   auto Src = LoadSourceGPR_WithOpSize(Op, Op->Src[0], SrcSize, Op->Flags, {.LoadData = false, .AllowUpperGarbage = SrcSize > DstSize});
